<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Thames frost fairs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">695–<a href="/years/1814">1814</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as frost fair</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-thames">River Thames</a> frost fairs were held on the frozen tideway of the River Thames in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in some winters, from at least the late 7th century until the early 19th. Most took place between the early 17th and early 19th centuries, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a>, when the river was wider and slower and was further impeded by the 19 piers of the medieval Old London Bridge. The first known fair was in 695, and the term was first used of the fair of 1608. During the Great Frost of 1683–84 the Thames was completely frozen for two months. The last fair began on 1 February 1814 and lasted four days, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> presses on the ice; the old bridge was removed in 1831.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-thames-frost-fairs.jpg" alt="River Thames frost fairs" width="330" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wyke · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Wyke-_Thames_frost_fair.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
